From 7ec56e9e475143acfecfffd59020464b60742b56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:49:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(share): the capacity read had to be able to leave the
 browser tab
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0021. The free qualifier produced a real engineering read — the binding
constraint with its basis, racks as found and after the ladder, the item that sets
the date — and then lost all of it when the tab closed.

That is not a cosmetic gap. The person who types seven numbers into a capacity
qualifier is an operations engineer. The person who signs off a five-figure study
is a director. The distance between them is a link, and there was not one, so every
read stopped at whoever happened to be at the keyboard.

/q/<token>

A permanent page carrying the engineering rather than a summary of it: the binding
constraint one click from its full reference page, what it takes to move and how
many weeks that takes in European retrofits, the intake completeness, and the same
disclosure the paid document carries — not a bankable number, not a design, not a
measurement of this asset, and a model not yet reconciled against any instrumented
site. A shareable page that softened any of that would be a brochure, and a
director who has read one brochure recognises the next.

The share block sits directly under the headline, not at the end. The moment to
forward something is the moment you have just read the thing worth forwarding.

THE BENCHMARK IS WHY IT GETS FORWARDED

The page says how the hall compares with every other hall the engine has seen:
"tap-off rating binds first in 11 of 34 halls; 19 of those deploy zero racks as
they stand." The only sentence on the page a competitor cannot write, and it costs
nothing to give away because giving it away is what produces the next row.

Same privacy floor as /api/insights, and a test asserts the two numbers are EQUAL —
lowering one would silently publish on a shared page what the public route
withholds. The query selects two columns, binding_constraint and racks_as_found,
and a test asserts nothing else can creep into it.

WHAT THE TESTS HOLD

  * No link when the row was not written. persist() now throws instead of logging
    and moving on, because a share link to a row that was never written is a 404
    sent to somebody's director.
  * The token is minted BEFORE the engine runs, so an unreachable engine still
    leaves a row to come back to, and the page says no read was produced rather
    than inventing one.
  * 18 random bytes, base64url. A short token on a page holding a customer's site
    figures is not a token.
  * /q/ disallowed for every crawler, page noindex — with a CI step that checks
    every token-addressed page at once, since this is the fourth one and the first
    that would have leaked a hall's own numbers.
  * Migration 0008 backfills before it constrains. A unique index added first
    refuses on the existing nulls and fails on exactly the deployments that already
    have data.

366 tests. tsc clean, build clean.

